<.shell title="Side navigation" current={:side_nav}>
  <h1>Side navigation</h1>
  <p class="lead">An area's pages, down the left of each page in it.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <Web.Layouts.area_side_nav area={sample_nav_area()} groups={sample_nav_groups()} />
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    On every page in a team's area, a member's area, and Admin. The app layout adds it to each of them.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Pages about one record, such as a member's attendance and groups. Use
      <a href={page_path(:tabs)}>tabs</a>
      under the record's title.
    </li>
    <li>Your settings, a single page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Pages come in groups. A group of two or more pages has a heading in small capitals; a group of one is a link on its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      The page you're on, or the section it's under, is marked with a straight bar. An area's first page, such as Dashboard, is marked only on itself.
    </li>
    <li>
      An amber <a href={page_path(:tag)}>tag</a>
      counts what waits for you on a page, such as proposed changes. Only for things to act on.
    </li>
    <li>
      Below 1024px the <a href={page_path(:top_bar)}>top bar</a>'s Menu button holds the same pages, under the same headings.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    The app layout's <code>area_side_nav</code>, built from <code>&lt;.side_nav&gt;</code>.
    <code>Web.Navigation</code>
    decides the area and its pages, so a new page is a line there.
  </p>
</.shell>
